 "facts": [
  {
   "claim": "Sargent (born 12 January 1856), a young American portrait painter based in Paris, painted Virginie Amélie Avegno Gautreau in 1883–84; it was not a commission",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portrait_of_Madame_X ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virginie_Am%C3%A9lie_Avegno_Gautreau"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Gautreau, born in New Orleans in 1859, was a celebrated Paris beauty ('la belle Mme Gautreau'), known for her pale skin, which she emphasised with lavender-coloured powder",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virginie_Am%C3%A9lie_Avegno_Gautreau"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Shown at the 1884 Paris Salon as 'Portrait de Mme ***' (no name); the sitter was nonetheless widely recognised",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portrait_of_Madame_X ; https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/12127"
  },
  {
   "claim": "As exhibited in 1884, the jewelled right strap of her dress had slipped off her shoulder (photographed in that state)",
   "source": "https://fashionhistory.fitnyc.edu/1884-sargent-madame-x/ ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portrait_of_Madame_X ; https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Madame_X_(original_hanging_strap).jpg"
  },
  {
   "claim": "The portrait provoked scandal and ridicule at the Salon; critics mocked it",
   "source": "https://fashionhistory.fitnyc.edu/1884-sargent-madame-x/ ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portrait_of_Madame_X ; https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/12127"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Her mother asked Sargent to withdraw the painting from the exhibition; he refused",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portrait_of_Madame_X ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virginie_Am%C3%A9lie_Avegno_Gautreau"
  },
  {
   "claim": "After the Salon (by autumn 1884), Sargent repainted the strap up onto her shoulder; X-rays confirm the change",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portrait_of_Madame_X ; https://fashionhistory.fitnyc.edu/1884-sargent-madame-x/"
  },
  {
   "claim": "The poor reception soon prompted Sargent to leave France for England; since Gautreau rejected the painting, he took it with him to London and kept it in his studio",
   "source": "https://fashionhistory.fitnyc.edu/1884-sargent-madame-x/ ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virginie_Am%C3%A9lie_Avegno_Gautreau ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portrait_of_Madame_X"
  },
  {
   "claim": "In 1916 (after Gautreau's death in 1915) he sold it to the Metropolitan Museum of Art, writing 'I suppose it is the best thing I have done', and asked the museum to disguise the sitter's name; it is known as Madame X",
   "source": "https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/12127 ; https://fashionhistory.fitnyc.edu/1884-sargent-madame-x/ ; https://www.metmuseum.org/perspectives/how-madame-x-came-to-the-met"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Hangs in the Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York (accession 16.53)",
   "source": "https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/12127"
  }
 ],
 "caption": "Un tirante de este vestido casi acaba con la carrera de John Singer Sargent.\n\nEn 1884 Sargent, un joven retratista en pleno ascenso en París, expuso en el Salón su retrato de Virginie Gautreau, titulado solo \"Madame ***\". Todo el mundo sabía quién era: una belleza nacida en Estados Unidos, famosa por su piel pálida y empolvada. En su versión, el tirante enjoyado derecho se le había caído del hombro.\n\nLos críticos se burlaron. Su madre le pidió que lo retirara. Él se negó. Después del Salón volvió a pintar el tirante en su sitio, pero poco después se fue de París a Londres y se llevó el cuadro con él.\n\nLo tuvo en su estudio más de treinta años. En 1916 se lo vendió al Museo Metropolitano de Arte: \"Supongo que es lo mejor que he hecho\". Pidió al museo que ocultara el nombre de ella, y por eso el mundo la conoce como Madame X.\n\nP.D. Una fotografía de 1884 todavía muestra el tirante caído.\n\n🖤 ¿De verdad era para tanto?",
 "caption_short": "Un tirante caído casi acaba con la carrera de Sargent. 👗",
